course: chemistry
lesson: 36
title: Olympiad: kitchen chemistry puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson turns the kitchen into a puzzle room: each question joins two ideas from the course, and many hide a trap in the wording.
explain: A good kitchen detective asks three things: is it a new substance or the same stuff in a new form, what got used up, and what changed - the warmth, the water, the air or the acid?
explain: Picture muffins coming out of the oven with green blueberries: too much baking soda turned the blueberry colour just as it turns red cabbage juice, and a squeeze of lemon would have kept them purple.
explain: Watch out for big talk: when Nan says "I've told you a million times, no foil in the microwave" she means many times, and when someone calls a pancake-thin sponge "a bit flat" they are playing it down.
explain: Every puzzle here is for thinking, not a recipe to try: hot sugar, boiling oil and kettles are a grown-up's job.
fact: Red cabbage juice is purple in neutral water, pink-red with acid and blue-green with baking soda; adding enough of the other one swings the colour back through purple.
fact: Blueberries hold the same kind of colour substance as red cabbage, so too much baking soda in the batter turns them greenish.
fact: Melting, freezing, boiling, dissolving and condensing can be undone, like chocolate that melts at about 34 °C and sets again; frying an egg, toasting bread and baking a cake cannot be undone.
fact: Water boils at 100 °C at sea level but at a lower temperature high on a mountain, so pasta cooks more slowly up there even when the water bubbles hard.
fact: Salty water freezes below 0 °C, so ice with salt gets much colder than ice alone, cold enough to freeze ice cream in a bag.
fact: Fresh pineapple stops jelly setting because it holds a substance that cuts gelatine; tinned pineapple has been heated, which spoils that substance, so jelly with tinned pineapple sets.
fact: Water that is too hot kills yeast, and dough in a cold place rises only slowly.
fact: Popcorn pops because water inside each kernel turns to steam until the shell bursts; old dried-out kernels pop badly.
fact: A cold can gets wet on the outside because water vapour from the air condenses on it; it is not leaking.
fact: Hot water dissolves sugar faster than cold water, and fine sugar dissolves faster than a lump; stirring helps too.
fact: A cut apple browns when air reaches it; lemon juice slows the browning, and cold slows it too.
fact: Baking powder holds both a dry acid and baking soda, so it fizzes with just water; plain baking soda needs an acid in the mix, such as lemon juice, yoghurt or buttermilk.
fact: In a fair test you change only one thing; if two things change at once, you cannot tell which one made the difference.
fact: Words from round the world: "nesh" (North of England and Midlands) means feeling the cold easily, "bussin'" (young US slang) means really tasty, and "fixin' to" (southern US) means about to.
exercise: A drop of vinegar turns red cabbage juice pink. You then add baking soda, a spoonful at a time, until nothing changes any more. Which colours do you see, in order? | pink, then purple, then blue-green | pink, then yellow, then purple again | pink, then blue-green, then back to pink | purple, then pink, then a deep red
exercise: Which is the odd one out: melting butter, boiling water, frying an egg, freezing juice? | Frying an egg: it can't be undone | Melting butter: it is the only one in a pan | Boiling water: it is the only one that bubbles | Freezing juice: it is the only one getting colder
exercise: A batch of muffins comes out with green blueberries. Which mix-up most likely caused it? | Too much baking soda in the batter | Far too little sugar in the batter | An oven much too hot for blueberries | Blueberries picked before they were ripe
exercise: At a mountain campsite the pasta water bubbles hard, yet the pasta takes far longer than at home. Why? | Up there, water boils at under 100 °C | The thin air steals heat from the pasta | Mountain water has no salt in it at all | The pan is too far from the Sun's warmth
exercise: Two bags of ice each hold a small bag of cream; one bag of ice has salt added. Which one can make ice cream? | The salty one, as ice with salt gets colder | The plain one, as salt would warm the cream | Both the same, as ice is always exactly 0 °C | Neither, as cream freezes only in a freezer
exercise: Jelly made with fresh pineapple stays runny, but jelly made with tinned pineapple sets. What is the best explanation? | Heating in the tin spoiled the gelatine-cutting substance | Tinned pineapple has more sugar, and sugar sets jelly | Fresh pineapple is too cold to let the jelly set | The tin adds gelatine to the fruit
exercise: Kai mixes yeast with water straight from the boiling kettle, then adds sugar and flour. Two hours later the dough is still flat. Most likely reason? | The very hot water killed the yeast | There wasn't enough flour in it | Sugar always stops yeast from working | Dough can only rise inside a fridge
exercise: Put these in the order they happen for a loaf: A crust browns in the oven, B yeast eats sugar, C dough puffs up, D gas is trapped in the gluten. | B, D, C, A | D, B, C, A | B, C, A, D | A, B, D, C
exercise: Popcorn kernels from the back of the cupboard, years old, barely pop. Why? | They dried out, so too little steam forms | They have lost all the oil from inside | Their shells went soft, so they can't burst | The starch inside has turned into sugar
exercise: Tom says the can from the fridge "is leaking" onto the table, but it was never opened. What is really going on? | Vapour in the air condenses on the cold can | Cold cola seeps slowly through the metal | Ice inside the can melts out through the seams | The fridge sprays water onto the cans
exercise: Which of these dissolves fastest? | Fine sugar in hot water, stirred | A sugar lump in hot water, not stirred | Fine sugar in cold water, not stirred | A sugar lump in cold water, stirred
exercise: One half of an apple gets lemon juice and goes in the fridge; the other half sits plain on a warm windowsill. Which stays pale longer? | The lemon half, as acid and cold both slow browning | The windowsill half, as sunlight stops the browning | The lemon half, but only the cold helps, not the lemon | Neither, as both halves touch the same air
exercise: A recipe uses plain baking soda to make a cake rise and also asks for buttermilk. Why the buttermilk? | Its acid reacts with the soda to make gas | It makes the cake taste of butter | Its fat feeds the soda so it grows bigger | It cools the batter so it rises slowly
exercise: Sam tests whether sugar dissolves faster in hot water. He puts fine sugar in hot water and a lump in cold water. What is wrong? | He changed two things at once | Nothing, that is a fair test of hot and cold | He should have used salt instead of sugar | He should have used one glass for both tests
exercise: Which of these is a new substance made in the kitchen? | The brown crust on the toast | The water drops on a pan lid | The melted butter in a pan | The cloud above the kettle
exercise: Chocolate melts at about 34 °C. Which is true? | It can melt in a warm hand and in your mouth, but not in the fridge | It melts only when it is heated above 100 °C | It melts in the fridge but never in your mouth | It needs a real flame to melt at all
test: «nan's told me a MILLION times no foil in the microwave, why tho» What is the real reason, and how often did Nan really say it? | Metal can spark in there; she said it many times | Foil makes the food taste of metal; a million times exactly | Foil melts into the food; she only said it once | Foil is safe; she just likes to moan about it
test: «ugh my sponge is a bit flat» - it is thin as a pancake, and she took it out after 10 of its 40 minutes. What does she really mean, and why did it happen? | Very flat; it came out too early and sank | Slightly flat; there was too much sugar | It's perfect; she's just being modest | Very flat; the eggs were too fresh
test: «bro these muffins r bussin but why r the blueberries GREEN, is it mould??» | No, too much baking soda changed their colour | Yes, green means mould, so bin the whole batch | No, blueberries are always green inside | Yes, the oven heat grew mould inside them
test: Liv says: "salt melts ice, so salt must make ice WARMER, so salt and ice can't make ice cream." Which answer spots her mistake? | Salty water freezes lower, so ice with salt gets colder, not warmer | She's right; the shops just use a big freezer | Salt warms the ice, but the cream is colder already | Salt freezes the cream if you sprinkle it on top
test: «me mam's dead nesh, she says it's FREEZING in here» - the thermometer says 19 °C. Will a glass of water on the table freeze? | No, she just feels the cold | Yes, freezing means below 0 °C | Only if salt goes in the glass | Yes, but only later at night
test: Grandad: "Back in my day we cooked pasta up a mountain, same ten minutes as at home, no bother." Is that likely? | No, water boils cooler up there, so it takes longer | Yes, water always boils at 100 °C everywhere | Yes, the thin air cooks food much faster | No, pasta can't cook properly above the clouds at all
test: Jaz is fixin' to make a jelly with fresh pineapple for a party in an hour. What should a mate tell her? | Use tinned pineapple, or it may not set | Add more pineapple to help it set faster | Put it in the oven so it sets quicker | Fine, fresh fruit always helps jelly to set
test: «it's literally boiling in here, I'm melting» says Ali. The kitchen is 26 °C. What is really happening? | Ali feels hot; nothing in the room is boiling | The kitchen air has reached 100 °C | Ali is slowly turning into a liquid | The water in the kettle boils all on its own in that heat
test: «popcorn's from 2019 lol, wanna try?» Only a few kernels pop. A friend blames the oil for being too cold. What is a better guess? | The old kernels dried out, so too little steam formed | The kernels have been too cold ever since 2019 | Old kernels grow a hard coat like metal | Popcorn only pops in the year it is picked
test: Ella wants to show her class the salt hidden in salty water, safely. Which plan works? | Pour it into a wide dish, leave it in the sun for days, then look for crystals | Filter it, and the salt stays on the paper | Freeze it, then pick out the salt cubes | Stir it hard until the salt sinks back down
test: «it's just a tiny splash, no big deal» says Ben, after hot caramel from his mum's pan landed on his hand. What is the wise move? | Cool it under cold running water and tell an adult | Ignore it; a splash that small can't hurt | Rub butter on it and carry on with the cooking | Pick the sticky caramel off quickly with nails
exam: Determine which of the following constitutes a chemical change. | The browning of bread in a toaster | The melting of butter in a warm pan | The dissolving of sugar in hot tea | The condensation of steam upon a cold lid
exam: A yeast dough was prepared with water at a scalding temperature and failed to rise. Identify the most probable cause. | The heat killed the yeast | The gluten strands were insufficiently kneaded | The flour contained too much protein | The sugar had been fully dissolved
exam: Red cabbage indicator turns blue-green upon the addition of a substance. Ascertain the substance. | Baking soda dissolved in water | Lemon juice | White wine vinegar | Plain tap water with nothing added
exam: State why tinned pineapple, unlike fresh pineapple, permits a gelatine jelly to set. | Heating during canning spoils the substance that cuts gelatine | Canning adds extra sugar, which hardens the jelly | Canning makes the fruit too cold to react | Tinned fruit contains no water whatsoever
